     A CVE list is the measured case and the first thing tried, because it is what the six
     failing pull requests had in common. Order-free on purpose: the same three CVEs in a
     different order are one finding, not three.
 
-    Falls back to a digest of the output with the noisy parts removed (line numbers, hex, paths),
-    so a finding with no CVE still gets a fingerprint worth comparing -- a coarse one, and the
-    tests say so rather than pretending it is exact.
+    THE MISFIRE OF 2026-09-13. This function used to fall back to a digest of ANY text, so
+    `echo hello` had a fingerprint, three repeated commands were "the same finding on three
+    targets", and the breaker locked bash and read -- the two tools a session needs to
+    investigate or fix anything. It locked a working session for hours and could not be cleared
+    from inside, because every clearing command was itself a bash call. R38: a guard that
+    refuses correct work is an outage.
+
+    A FINDING IS SOMETHING A DIAGNOSTIC PRODUCED. Nothing below is a finding, however many times
+    it repeats, and returning None is what makes that true:
+
+        a command that succeeded           (no error, no failing test, no gate verdict)
+        a shell builtin's echo of its input
+        a blank or near-blank result
+
+    Anything else keeps a coarse digest with the noisy parts removed (addresses, line:col,
+    timestamps), sorted, so the same failure on two machines still compares equal. The tests say
+    it is coarse rather than pretending it is exact.
     """
     cves = sorted(set(_CVE.findall(text or "")))
     if cves:
         return "cve:" + ",".join(cves)
+
     cleaned = []
     for line in (text or "").splitlines():
         line = re.sub(r"0x[0-9a-fA-F]+", "", line)  # addresses
@@ -60,7 +97,16 @@ def fingerprint(text: str) -> str:
         line = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", line).strip()
         if line:
             cleaned.append(line)
+    if not cleaned:
