+# Gate: breaker-enf
+
+*`breaker-enforcement` · R38, incident 2026-09-13 · planes: ci*
+
+The fingerprint of a tool's output is the stable identity of a FINDING, never the bytes it printed: output carrying no finding -- a successful command, a shell builtin echoing its input, a blank result -- yields no fingerprint and can never lock, however many times it repeats, while the same CVE set on three targets still locks. One rule, in bin/idp-circuit-breaker
+
+This page is generated by `bin/idp-gate-demo` from `rules.yaml`. The output below is what the gate itself printed when the generator ran it; it is not a description of what the gate does.
+
+## Run it yourself
+
+```bash
+python3 bin/idp-breaker-fingerprint
+```
+
+## Refused
+
+Fixture: `tests/fixtures/breaker-fingerprint/bad/real-cves.txt`
+
+Exit code `1` (the registry expects non-zero). ✓
+
+```
+ok     breaker-fingerprint  real-cves.txt -> cve:CVE-2026-13221,CVE-2026-42496,CVE-2026-8376
+```
+
+## Permitted
+
+Fixture: `tests/fixtures/breaker-fingerprint/good/noop-repeated.txt`
+
+Exit code `0` (the registry expects zero). ✓
+
+```
+ok     breaker-fingerprint  noop-repeated.txt carries no finding; repeating it cannot lock
+```
+
+## The registry's own lines
+
+- when it passes: a result carrying no finding cannot lock, and a proved pattern still locks
+- when it refuses: output carrying no finding gets a fingerprint, so repeated no-op commands trip N=3 and lock bash and read -- the two tools a session needs to fix anything. Measured 2026-09-13: a session issued the same command repeatedly, the third tripped the lock, and every command that could have cleared it was itself a bash call. R38 -- a guard that refuses correct work is an outage.
+
+Origin: incident 2026-09-13, the breaker locked bash and read for hours
